        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Phenakite is a beryllium silicate, one of the few minerals in which beryllium is the only metal cation. It forms at high temperature in beryllium-rich granitic pegmatites, greisens and mica schists, often through alteration of beryl when aluminium-poor late fluids destabilise it. It also occurs in the emerald deposits of the Urals, with chrysoberyl and emerald. Colourless, sometimes faintly yellowish or pinkish, it owes its name to the Greek “phenax”, deceiver, as it was mistaken for quartz.",
            "Trigonal system. Short rhombohedral or prismatic crystals, often penetration twins. Imperfect cleavage; conchoidal fracture. Hardness 7.5 to 8; specific gravity 2.93 to 3.00; bright vitreous lustre; refractive index 1.654 to 1.670; birefringence 0.016 (uniaxial positive). Brighter than quartz thanks to its higher refractive index, it is also distinguished by greater hardness. Faceted stones, often very clean, rarely exceed a few carats and appeal mainly to collectors. Some have a yellow or pink colour that fades in light.",
            "Russia: the Urals (Takovaya, the Malyshevo emerald mines), the type locality described in 1833, historic crystals. Brazil (Minas Gerais, São Miguel de Piracicaba), Madagascar, Myanmar (Mogok), Sri Lanka, Namibia, Nigeria, Tanzania, Norway, Switzerland and the United States (Colorado, Mount Antero; Maine).",
            "A stone of spiritual uplift and intuition, often cited among the most “high-vibration” stones in crystal healing, said to open one to meditation and inner awareness. It is associated with the crown and third-eye chakras and the sign of Gemini. Cleansing in water; recharging in moonlight or gentle sunlight."
